\begin{tikzpicture}\begin{axis}[width=6.5cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={-{Stealth}},xmin=0,xmax=9,ymin=0,ymax=24,xtick={0,1,...,9},ytick={0,4,...,24},grid=both,clip=false,scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\small,/pgf/number format/fixed},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$\$ (100s)$}]
\addplot[thick,blue!70!black,domain=0:9,samples=100] {-x*x+9*x};
\addplot[thick,red!75!black,dashed,domain=0:9] {x+7};
\addplot[only marks,mark=*,mark size=2pt] coordinates {(1,8) (7,14)};
\end{axis}\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The curve is above the line between the crossing points at $x=1$ and $x=7$.
So the stall makes a profit for $1<x<7$ crates.
The largest whole number that still breaks even or better is $x=7$ crates.
